# A resource server binds the payment to its own invoice reference, and the client
# honors it.
#
# Every payment to this server lands in one address, so the transfer alone does not
# say which order it settles. The offer therefore names a memo, the client copies it
# into the transaction it signs, and it travels on chain with the money.
#
# extra.memo is not a gno invention. The shipped SVM mechanism defines the same key
# for the same reason — a seller-defined UTF-8 string carrying a payment reference
# such as an invoice id, so reconciliation needs no unique deposit address per payer
# — and caps it at the same 256 bytes. Canton spells its example "invoice-2024-001".
# Where gno differs is only the carrier: tm2 signs a memo as part of the transaction,
# so there is no separate instruction to attach.
#
# The settlement is the assertion. The facilitator compares the transaction's memo
# against the one the requirements name and refuses a mismatch, so a payment that
# settles is proof the client read extra.memo and signed it in. A client that ignored
# it is answered invalid_exact_gno_payload_memo_mismatch — the same refusal SVM
# publishes as invalid_exact_solana_payload_memo_mismatch — and nothing is broadcast.
adduser seller

gnoland start

gnokey query bank/balances/$seller_user_addr
stdout '1000000000ugnot'

x402facilitator
x402seller invoice.json $seller_user_addr

exec node $X402_BUYER
stdout '"status":200'

# The resource was served, so honoring the memo did not cost the client the purchase.
stdout 'export ready'

# The payment settled: the memo the offer named was in the signed transaction.
stdout '"transaction":"[0-9a-zA-Z]'
! stdout 'memo_mismatch'

gnokey query bank/balances/$seller_user_addr
stdout '1000250000ugnot'

-- invoice.json --
{
  "route": "GET /orders/1042/export",
  "price": { "asset": "ugnot", "amount": "250000" },
  "description": "Order data export",
  "mimeType": "application/json",
  "memo": "inv-2026-08-1042",
  "body": {
    "order": 1042,
    "status": "export ready"
  }
}
